A||N||9||Slapnutz2000||Pioneer DEH45-DH/Crutchfield Installation||||1||
Z||000000||Slapnutz2000||07-14-2000||11:56 AM||||I bought the wiring harness that will be needed for me to finally install the new CD player. Question is, is this harness a direct plug and play or do I have to crimp and/or solder (sp?)<P>TIA!||||reg||1
Z||000001||286SE'S||07-14-2000||12:45 PM||||If you got the same adapter I did (for Pioneer DEH-76DH) You will see that what you bought is a connector that plugs into the socket that was plugged into the Fiero's stock radio. The other end is just a bunch of wires that you must match to the wires from your Pioneer. There were directions with mine and matching the wires (using the Pioneer install manual) was easy. I soldered and used shrink tubing over my connections. Everthing worked first time. There is a stray wire attached to the old radio (orange and white) that +12 (always hot) for the clock and station presets.  Anybody else have comments on this orange/white connector?<BR>One problem I had with the Pioneer head unit was: The bezel hole was too small to go over the radio and still allow the faceplate to detach for security. I used a dremel tool to carefully remove that 1/8" lip from the openings' sides (not cutting into the silver painted area). Perfect fit now.<BR>Fantastic radio, CD player NEVER skipped once. Take my advice and get the SR-77 steering wheel remote while it is still available. It's worth the $39.00<BR>Terry||||reg||1
Z||000002||Black88GT||07-14-2000||01:09 PM||||That orange and white wire, is that the one that has the "clip" connector? If it is, DO NOT CONNECT IT! When I put my radio in, I saw that clip and connected it with the other end coming from the car, they fit perfectly so I figured that had to be right.  Well, after that my battery kept on dying. I disconnected the trunk light, but that wasn't it.  Come to find out it was that switch! I found out when I turned the car off, took my keys out, and the radio still worked! However, it won't hold the time, it keeps on resetting it.  <P>||||reg||11
Z||000003||Raydar||07-14-2000||02:55 PM||||The original "small" GM connector has two wires. One is for the light dimmer (clock dims down when the headlights/dashlights are on. The other is for the clock and station preset memory. I forget which one is missing from the small "Crutchfield" harness, but I believe it needs to be there. It <I>does</I> show up in the large plug, which is the wrong place. I have delayed the installation of my own Pioneer, mostly because of this. (Don't really have the time to chase it and fix it, just now. Need to get a pin for the small plug.)<BR>If you have the HELM manual, it has the correct pinouts. The Haynes might, also, but I haven't checked it. Good luck and be careful. After I figure out what's what, I'll post some info. <BR>Won't be this weekend, though. Jimmy Buffett's in town. I won't be in any condition...<P>------------------<P>Raydar<BR>88 Formula T-Top <BR>88 Coupe (ISO 3800)<P>"Some mornings, it just isn't worth<BR>chewing through the restraints."<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 07-14-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Standard||07-14-2000||04:22 PM||||hmm.. I just installed my Pioneer DEH-P5200 about a month ago.. I remember an orange and white wire but I'll have to pull the deck out to remember what I did with that wire, tho..<P>This was my first stereo install and I had no problems whatsoever.  I used insulated crimps for the wiring harness.  Everything worked the first time, so I was happy.  Now I just have to hook up the DFS alarm..  <P>------------------<BR>Mike -- Golden Valley, MN<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 147,000 miles and counting!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||BN Boomer||07-14-2000||05:09 PM||||Wow, that really sucks. I always thought that the "quick connect" wiring harness adapter did just that. Talk about misleading. Guess now I'm even happier that I save a few bucks by skipping the Pioneer unit and going with an OEM type Delco Pontiac CD player. It was a totally bolt in installation. I'm sure it doesn't offer the overall performance of the Pioneer units, but for $78.20 including a true "plug 'n play" wiring harness adapter and shipping, it's good enough. How could I possibly go wrong?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/boomers_fiero_files/85gt1.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by BN Boomer (edited 07-14-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||FieroGTGirl||07-14-2000||08:54 PM||||BNBoomer~~~<P>That is identical to my stereo in our 2000 Grand Am GT.  Don't give Coconut any ideas, PLEASE!!  Next thing I know he is gonna put the Grand Am's CD player in the Fiero. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FieroGTGirl_sig3.gif"> ||||reg||12
Z||000007||Raydar||07-15-2000||01:08 AM||||Boomer. Nice radio. I was *attempting* to buy one like that, but couldn't get the guy to return my calls. (Was a business, not an auction.) <P>Kills me how some people are in business to sell a product. They have a buyer that is chomping at the bit, then they don't bother calling you back. After you call them 3-4 times. <BR>Screw 'em. Bought the Pioneer.<P>Sorry. &lt;climbing down from soapbox&gt;<P>Kristina...  I think the CD player would be a perfect fit in the Fiero.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> <P>------------------<P>Raydar<BR>88 Formula T-Top <BR>88 Coupe (ISO 3800)<P>"Some mornings, it just isn't worth<BR>chewing through the restraints."<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 07-15-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||tgowens||07-15-2000||02:10 AM||||Gee <B>Boomer</B>, that looks just like mine!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/my_interior.jpg">   (I think mine looks more "expensive" tho -- you got a h**l of a deal.<P>Kristina, keep a watch on that Grand Am and don't let Coconut near it!!! (hey, cool signature, by the way!)<P>------------------<BR>     '87 Fiero GT<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.tgowens.com/3D_sig.gif">            <BR><A HREF="http://www.tgowens.com/fiero" TARGET=_blank>www.tgowens.com/fiero</A>||||reg||1
Z||000009||DiggityDan||07-15-2000||05:30 PM||||Since my original tape player was FUBAR'd, I just cut that small connector on the tape player off as far back as I could and then used that to attach to the new wiring harness. Perfect fit and everthing works perfectly.||||reg||1
